WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:02.870
Hey guys, Doctor Berg here.

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:04.100
I have a question.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.490
Can't sleep?

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:06.490
Do this.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:10.290
Number one) always sleep on your right side.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.500
Why?

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.240
Because the heart is on the left side and
the liver is on the right side and so many

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:22.090
people have either a fatty liver, or a congested
liver, and when you sleep on your left side,

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.910
the way the liver can compress on the heart.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:29.369
It's much better if the heart is sitting on
top of the liver sleeping on your right side.

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:32.220
You'll find that your breathing will be better,
your sleep will be better, if you sleep on

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.379
the right side, okay?

00:00:33.379 --> 00:00:34.620
That's number one.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.290
Number two) slow your breathing down.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:41.400
What you want to do is you want to focus on
your breathing.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.390
Here's what happens when your adrenals are
over-stressed.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.830
The breathing is kind of like this.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:52.479
I'm exaggerating, but the exhalation is shorter
than the inhalation, so what you want to do

00:00:52.479 --> 00:00:58.390
is you want to sync your breathing with an
equal amount of inhalation and exhalation.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.650
About four to five seconds each, okay?

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:05.460
It's kind of like you're going to slowly count
to four or five and you're breathing in.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:10.560
One, two, three, four.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.680
One, two, three, four as you're breathing
out.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.150
So you're slowly breathing in and slowly breathing
out.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:23.540
As you're trying to sleep, I find that really
helps your sleep okay?

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:28.450
Some people all of a sudden they catch themselves
holding their breath when they're trying to

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:29.450
sleep.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.820
This is a really good thing to focus on as
you drift off into a sleep.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.659
Okay, number three) bed by 10:30 at least.

00:01:36.659 --> 00:01:39.730
Maybe a little bit earlier because here's
the problem.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:48.010
There's a circadian rhythm that occurs and
when you go to bed past 10:30, let's say 11:30

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:51.150
or 12 especially, you missed the make/break
point.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:56.190
In other words, you may have to wait up to
an hour to even 90 minutes before you catch

00:01:56.190 --> 00:02:00.409
the next wave because these hormones go in
cycles up and down.

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:04.420
If you missed out on that circadian wave,
all of a sudden, you can't sleep.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.060
You maybe already know that.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.039
You might get tired around 9:30 and like,
"Man, if I just went to bed now, I could just

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:12.230
sleep," then all of a sudden you wake up.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:13.600
That's what I'm talking about.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.709
You want to get to bed by 10:30 because the
optimum time of that circadian wave is about

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:19.970
10:45/11 o'clock.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:22.450
That's when your body drifts off into a sleep.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:26.680
I consistently go to bed at that time and
I can drift off very fast, but if I go to

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.760
bed at 12, I'll be laying there for an hour,
okay?

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:31.760
That's one thing.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.600
Number four) your pulse rate.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:39.390
If you ever go to bed and you hear your pulse
rate on the pillow like boom, boom, boom,

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:43.210
boom, what that means is that you're deficient
in potassium.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:49.020
If you're deficient in potassium, your heart
will beat louder and harder and a higher pulse

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.020
rate.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:57.120
Normal pulse rate, 72, but you want it 65/60,
so if it's too high, what you need to do is

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.220
you need to start eating more vegetables because
